---
// The landing — first moment of the cabinet of curiosities.
// True black. The nerve is already crossing the screen and already
// drifting: the site is mid thought and the visitor interrupted it.
// Centred: the name + tagline as one block, and a quote at rest below
// it. After ~3s, or on first pointer move / scroll, the mark block
// walks to the top left — permanence by contrast of behaviour, not
// size. Nothing fades to nothing; everything just gets quieter, per
// prototype/field.html (#mark, #meta), the working reference this
// ports faithfully.
//
// Meta is Eduardo's own copy, taken verbatim from that prototype. The
// tagline was revised directly by Eduardo in this session, replacing
// the prototype's original ("data · AI · the human experience").
//
// The nerve used to be drawn here, but it's really the field's nerve,
// not the landing's — Field.astro now owns it (same canvas, always
// present from load) and this section sits transparently on top so it
// shows through underneath the wordmark.
//
// The quote itself (#frag-a / #frag-b) went from one fixed sentence to
// a rotating, decaying system per a second opinion's reference
// prototype (sopaldalanding.zip) -- the manifesto line shown first
// (Eduardo's thesis, no source, still a headline), then rotating
// through the same 50-fragment pool Field.astro's continuous world
// already uses (src/data/fragments.ts, one source of truth), each one
// resting a while and then physically coming apart -- float, fall,
// explode, corrode, cycling in that order -- while the next one is
// already rising in the other layer. Fragment decay is load-bearing
// per CLAUDE.md ("the Wittgenstein argument running as physics, not a
// decoration"); this just brings that same idea to the first thing a
// visitor sees, instead of confining it to the field. Touching the
// quote holds it against its own death, same as the reference's own
// framing ("tocarla la sostiene, para la muerte"). Entering the field
// stops the rotation entirely -- Field.astro's own many fragments take
// over that role at that point, so there's no second decaying-quote
// system running underneath it.
---

<section class="landing" id="landing" aria-label="Eduardo Sopalda">
  <div class="top-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="mark">
    <h1 class="wordmark">
      <span class="wordmark-eduardo">EDUARDO</span> <span class="wordmark-sopalda">sopalda</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="tagline">DATA | AI <span class="amp">&amp;</span> The human experience</p>
  </div>

  <p class="purpose" id="frag-a"></p>
  <p class="purpose" id="frag-b" hidden></p>
  <p class="frag-source" id="frag-source" aria-hidden="true"></p>

  <p class="meta">Barcelona<br /><a href="#" id="li">LinkedIn</a></p>

  <button type="button" class="cue" id="cue" aria-label="Continue">
    <span class="cue-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="cue-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="cue-word" aria-hidden="true">Continue</span>
  </button>
</section>

<style>
  /* Transparent on purpose — Field.astro's canvas sits fixed behind
     this section and needs to show through. .landing only exists now
     to host the text overlay and give it a stacking context above
     the field (z-index below, on .mark/.purpose/.meta/.cue). */
  .landing {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100svh;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .landing .cue,
  .landing #li,
  .landing .purpose {
    /* .purpose needs this too now -- the hold-to-save-the-quote
       gesture is a pointerdown listener on it, which silently never
       fired at all while it inherited pointer-events: none from
       .landing above (confirmed directly: a simulated click at its
       exact coordinates had no effect, and decay kept running on
       schedule as if never touched). */
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  /* Each of the three blocks (mark, purpose, meta) positions and
     animates itself independently — anchored at its own left/top +
     translate, never nested inside a container that ALSO transforms.
     Compounding a parent transform with child transforms was an
     earlier bug: the whole group shot off-screen because the math
     assumed a center-pinned parent that didn't exist. */

  /* A frozen bar across the top, per Eduardo's note, to give the
     corner mark a persistent home rather than floating text -- room
     for whatever else lands up there later, too. Champagne, not the
     nerve's actual --gold: the design system reserves --gold as one
     scarce accent ("nowhere else"), so a loud gold bar would compete
     with the nerve itself. This reads as light gold without spending
     that scarcity -- an ambient wash, fading out by 70% of its own
     height, not a hard-edged colored block. Only visible in the field
     state; behind .mark, not part of it, so it doesn't inherit the
     mark's own position/size transition. */
  .top-bar {
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: clamp(4.5rem, 12vh, 7rem);
    /* Confirmed rendering correctly (verified in a screenshot -- it's
       there), but Eduardo still couldn't see it, which means "technically
       present" isn't good enough at 0.14 peak opacity -- raised
       meaningfully so it's unmistakable rather than something you have
       to know to look for. */
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(230, 203, 155, 0.32) 0%, rgba(230, 203, 155, 0.12) 55%, transparent 100%);
    /* Was 0 on the splash, full only after entering the field -- a
       design critique noted the two states didn't read as one system
       because of it ("bring a quieter version onto the splash").
       Faint from the first frame, then strengthens once in the field,
       same wash throughout instead of switching on. */
    opacity: 0.4;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
    transition: opacity 2.4s var(--ease);
  }

  body[data-landing='field'] .top-bar {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .top-bar {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* The mark: name + tagline, travelling together as one unit — this
     is what prototype/field.html's #mark does (one block, one
     transform), not two independently-animated lines. */
  .mark {
    /* Fixed, not absolute -- pinned to the viewport itself rather than
       .landing's document-flow box, so it holds its position (a frozen
       bar, per Eduardo's note) no matter what: dragging the nerve on a
       touch device shouldn't be able to carry it away even if that
       gesture ever triggers real page scroll. */
    position: fixed;
    left: 50%;
    top: 38%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    text-align: center;
    z-index: 1;
    /* The walk to the corner is the metamorphosis, not a UI snap --
       slow and unhurried on purpose, per Eduardo's note.
       text-align can't be smoothly interpolated at all -- it was
       switching center -> left the instant the field-state class
       applied, at t=0, while the block was still centre-screen and
       full size. That snap read as a "bump" partway through what
       should have been one continuous motion. transition-delay can't
       smooth a non-animatable property, but it CAN defer the jump: set
       to fire only once the position/size transition has actually
       finished, so the snap lands after the block is already small and
       in the corner, where it's imperceptible. */
    transition: left 4.2s var(--ease-slow), top 4.2s var(--ease-slow), transform 4.2s var(--ease-slow), text-align 0s 4.2s;
  }

  body[data-landing='field'] .mark {
    left: clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    top: clamp(1.25rem, 5vh, 3rem);
    transform: translate(0, 0);
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* Two names, two faces: Eduardo in Fraunces — the display system's
     "who he is" — and Sopalda in Glacial Indifference, because the
     surname is round-letter-heavy and reads easiest leaning into that
     rather than fighting it. Both start out of focus and resolve in,
     Sopalda a half-beat behind, so the eye lands on the given name
     first and the round one gathers after. */
  .wordmark {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1;
    /* "EDUARDO" and "sopalda" are two spans with a plain space between
       them -- normal wrap applies by default, and at the hero size the
       combined width doesn't fit one line, so it starts wrapped and
       only becomes one line once shrunk enough mid-transition. Traced
       it directly (measuring each span's left edge across the whole
       transition, not guessing): wrapped the entire hero phase, single
       line from partway through onward -- a real reflow, the same
       family of bug as the tagline's text-wrap: balance fix. Forcing
       one line here instead; the responsive clamp()s already bring the
       hero size down substantially on narrow viewports specifically so
       this fits without needing to wrap. */
    white-space: nowrap;
    /* The literal space between the two spans is a bare text node, so
       it renders at the H1'S OWN font-size -- which was never set, so
       it fell back to the browser default (~16px) regardless of how
       enormous the two words next to it were. That's why the gap
       read as an accident rather than a designed lockup: a fixed
       ~8px gap next to letters 60-160px tall. Shrinking the h1's own
       font-size to near-zero makes that stray space negligible, so
       the real gap below (margin-left on sopalda, in ITS OWN em
       units) is the only thing controlling it -- and now scales
       proportionally as the whole mark shrinks into the corner. Kept
       the actual space character in markup rather than deleting it,
       so the accessible name still reads as two words, not one
       concatenated string. */
    font-size: 1px;
  }

  .wordmark-eduardo {
    background-clip: text;
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
    opacity: 0;
    filter: blur(18px);
    animation: resolve-focus 2.4s var(--ease) forwards;
    font-family: var(--font-wordmark-given);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    /* A lot bigger, per Eduardo's note -- the name should arrive at
       full weight before the metamorphosis shrinks it to a mark. */
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 7.5vw, 6.5rem);
    /* Was a bronze-to-flesh sheen -- flesh is a skin tone, not a
       metal, so EDUARDO never joined the same "precious metal"
       family sopalda's gold peak belongs to; a design critique
       called this out directly ("gold sheen only on sopalda... the
       given name becomes a caption"). Champagne instead: still a
       visibly quieter shine than sopalda's gold (per the same
       critique's "weaker on EDUARDO"), but from the same warm-metal
       family rather than a different material entirely. Peak band
       narrowed (45-65% to 44-56%) so the shine reads as a crisper
       highlight, not a slow, muddy fade. */
    background-image: linear-gradient(100deg, var(--bronze-deep) 0%, var(--bronze) 44%, var(--champagne) 50%, var(--bronze) 56%, var(--bronze-deep) 100%);
    animation-delay: 0.2s;
    transition: font-size 4.2s var(--ease-slow);
  }

  .wordmark-sopalda {
    font-family: var(--font-wordmark);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(3.6rem, 11.5vw, 10rem);
    /* No background-image/clip here any more -- this element carries
       no direct text of its own now (see the script below), and
       leaving the gradient on it turned it into a plain, unclipped
       rectangle painted BEHIND the letters -- confirmed directly: the
       letters were individually correct, but a solid gradient block
       painted on the parent under them made the whole word
       unreadable. Each letter below carries the actual gradient as
       its own slice, not the parent -- see the script for the current
       stops (a near-white nacar-sheen spike was tried here for a
       "specular highlight," on top of the critique's "muddy, not
       metal" note; in practice it read as a hard chrome flash, cheap
       rather than precious. Pulled back to a plain gold peak). */
    /* Optical kerning between the lockup's two halves -- was a bare
       text-space at the h1's own (now ~0) font-size, an accident, not
       a designed gap. In em units against sopalda's own size, so it
       scales with the same font-size transition during the
       metamorphosis instead of jumping. */
    margin-left: 0.12em;
    transition: font-size 4.2s var(--ease-slow);
  }

  /* sopalda carries no text of its own any more -- a second opinion's
     reference prototype gives the surname a scatter-then-gather flourish
     on dock ("sopalda ~6.8s, con un desvío") that EDUARDO doesn't get,
     echoing this file's own established theme: EDUARDO resolves first
     and directly, sopalda gathers a half-beat behind. Split into
     per-letter <b>s by the script below so each one can wander
     independently. background-clip: text on the parent would have
     nothing left to clip once its text moves into these children, so
     each letter carries its own copy of the gradient instead -- WAAPI-
     animating opacity/transform on a letter promotes it to its own
     compositing layer, which breaks a shared parent clip the same way
     it broke the hero quote's gradient earlier; confirmed with the
     same kind of isolated test before building this for real rather
     than assuming it twice. The letter's own background-size/position
     are set in PERCENTAGES (by the script), not fixed pixels, so the
     gradient slice stays correctly aligned as font-size continuously
     shrinks through the dock transition -- percentages recompute
     against the letter's own current box on every frame; pixels
     wouldn't have.

     No CSS rule for the letters themselves here on purpose: Astro
     scopes this component's <style> block by stamping a data-astro-cid
     attribute onto template elements at build time, but these <b>s are
     created at runtime by the script below and never get that
     attribute, so a scoped rule here would silently never match them
     (confirmed directly -- plain black text on a literal rectangular
     gradient background instead of clipped, transparent text). Every
     property they need is set as an inline style in the script
     instead, same as Field.astro's own dynamically-created elements
     already have to be (see that file's header comment for the same
     failure mode). */

  body[data-landing='field'] .wordmark-eduardo {
    /* Was 0.7-0.85rem -- reasonable next to the old, smaller hero size,
       but the hero got "a lot bigger" per Eduardo's earlier note and
       this didn't scale with it, so the shrink ended up far more
       extreme than intended and the corner mark read as too small to
       work as a persistent header. */
    font-size: clamp(0.9rem, 1.3vw, 1.05rem);
  }

  body[data-landing='field'] .wordmark-sopalda {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 1.9vw, 1.5rem);
  }

  @keyframes resolve-focus {
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      filter: blur(0);
    }
  }

  /* A specimen tag, not a headline: IBM Plex Mono is already the
     design system's face for labels and catalogue numbers, so setting
     the tagline there — tracked-out caps — reads as one system with
     the rest of the cabinet, instead of a third unrelated typeface
     competing with the two the wordmark already uses. Width comes
     from letter-spacing, not a stretched sentence. */
  .tagline {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(0.8rem, 1.7vw, 1.2rem);
    color: var(--champagne);
    letter-spacing: 0.32em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-align: center;
    /* text-wrap: balance was the real second source of the "bump" --
       found by sampling position every 200ms through the transition
       and seeing a real reflow (width dropping ~70px in one step,
       position briefly reversing direction) right around the
       midpoint, unrelated to the text-align fix above. As font-size
       shrinks continuously, this line crosses the width where the
       browser's line-balancing algorithm picks a different break point
       -- a discrete layout event in the middle of what should be
       continuous motion. The narrow-viewport letter-spacing reduction
       just below was already tuned to keep this on one line, so
       nowrap just makes that explicit and removes the wrap entirely. */
    white-space: nowrap;
    opacity: 0;
    animation: resolve-quiet 1.6s var(--ease) forwards;
    animation-delay: 1.6s;
    /* Same text-align issue as .mark above, and more visible here since
       this element has width: 100% -- a real box wider than its own
       text, not just a shrink-wrapped one -- so the center -> left snap
       produced a genuine, noticeable horizontal jump instead of a
       subtle one. Deferred the same way, to land only once the block
       has finished moving. */
    transition: font-size 4.2s var(--ease-slow), margin-top 4.2s var(--ease-slow), letter-spacing 4.2s var(--ease-slow), text-align 0s 4.2s;
  }

  body[data-landing='field'] .tagline {
    font-size: clamp(0.55rem, 0.75vw, 0.65rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* A bare "&" in tracked uppercase mono reads cheap next to two
     considered typefaces, per the design critique -- a small italic
     serif flourish (borrowed from the display face, not a third
     family) instead, the standard editorial answer to the same
     problem. The word itself is untouched -- Eduardo's own line, only
     the glyph's style changes. */
  .tagline .amp {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.2em;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  /* Full tracking only works with room to breathe. Under ~30rem the
     wide letter-spacing that reads as a specimen tag on desktop pushes
     this onto three ragged lines instead — found by actually
     screenshotting a phone-width viewport, not by assumption. */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .tagline {
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    }

    /* The hero wordmark's clamp() floor (2.4rem / 3.6rem) is a fixed
       size that the 7.5vw/11.5vw term never actually reaches below
       roughly a 500px viewport -- so on every real phone the combined
       "EDUARDO sopalda" rendered at a constant ~408px wide, wider than
       the phone itself. white-space: nowrap (needed to stop the
       2-line/1-line reflow bug above) then meant that overflow was
       silently clipped by .landing's overflow: hidden instead of
       wrapping -- the first/last letters were never actually there to
       animate, just cut off outside the viewport the whole time.
       Confirmed by measuring the rendered rects directly at a 390px
       viewport: eduardo started at x:-9px, mark ended at right:399px,
       both outside the 390px frame. Lowering the floor here lets the
       vw term do its job and scale the hero size down to actually fit
       narrow phones instead of fighting the viewport. */
    .wordmark-eduardo {
      font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 7.5vw, 6.5rem);
    }

    .wordmark-sopalda {
      font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 11.5vw, 10rem);
    }
  }

  /* The quote at rest -- one of #frag-a/#frag-b visible at a time,
     the other hidden, JS crossfading between them (see script below).
     Both share this one rule, stacked at the exact same spot, so
     swapping which is visible reads as one continuous element rather
     than two things swapping places. */
  .purpose {
    /* Fixed for the same reason as .mark above -- the permanent
       message shouldn't be able to drift with a page scroll a
       nerve-drag might trigger. */
    position: fixed;
    left: 50%;
    /* Was 56% -- almost exactly the nerve's own resting band. A
       friend's design critique caught it precisely: "the purpose line
       sits on top of the nerve... type and graphic occupy the same
       horizontal band and cancel each other" -- confirmed directly by
       screenshotting the idle hero state, where the line visibly ran
       straight through "first... then you decide". The nerve's own
       amplitude was later tightened too (see Field.astro), but keeping
       real vertical clearance here as well costs nothing. */
    top: 74%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    margin: 0;
    /* Was nowrap, fine for one fixed sentence tuned to fit a line --
       the rotation now pulls from Field.astro's full fragment pool,
       and several of those run 80-90 characters ("Ask five people
       across one company..."). Centred wrapping across 1-3 lines,
       same as Field.astro's own .frag-quote, rather than forcing every
       fragment to fit the manifesto's exact width. */
    max-width: min(85vw, 34rem);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    /* WONK was 1 -- Fraunces' axis for its wonky, hand-lettered
       alternate letterforms, deliberately irregular by design. Fine
       for a display headline having fun with itself; wrong for a
       sentence about trust and rigor, and called out directly as
       looking bad here. 0 uses the regular (non-wonky) forms; SOFT
       (terminal roundness) is unrelated and left as is. */
    font-variation-settings: 'SOFT' 50, 'WONK' 0;
    /* A touch bigger than before (was 0.9-1.25rem) -- the critique's
       "treat it as a headline" note applies to whichever line is
       currently resting here, not only the manifesto. */
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.8vw, 1.4rem);
    line-height: 1.34;
    /* Flat colour, not the wordmark's gradient -- tried the gradient
       first (background-clip: text on the parent, letters as plain
       nested spans with no background of their own), and a static
       side-by-side render confirmed it CAN work that way. But the
       moment a letter is actually animated -- opacity/transform via
       Web Animations, needed for both the reveal and the decay
       physics below -- the browser promotes it to its own compositing
       layer, and once isolated like that it paints with its OWN
       (nonexistent) background and inherited transparent colour
       instead of sharing the parent's clipped gradient paint: the
       text just disappears the instant animation starts. Confirmed
       directly (computed style showed opacity:1 and the right rect,
       yet nothing rendered) before landing on this. Oxide-lit, the
       same flat colour Field.astro's own decaying fragments use (a
       second opinion's reference prototype uses this same muted
       bronze-rust for its own quotes, not flesh's much lighter
       pink-tan) -- consistent with this being a preview of that same
       system, not a mismatched one-off. */
    color: var(--oxide-lit);
    text-align: center;
    z-index: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: left 4.2s var(--ease-slow), top 4.2s var(--ease-slow), transform 4.2s var(--ease-slow), font-size 4.2s var(--ease-slow);
  }

  /* The manifesto specifically -- Eduardo's own thesis, singled out by
     the design critique as "the best piece of copy on the page...
     treat it as a headline" -- gets a warmer, brighter flat tone than
     the rotating fragments that follow it, so it still reads as a cut
     above the rest even without the gradient. */
  .purpose.is-manifesto {
    color: var(--champagne);
  }

  .purpose .word {
    display: inline-block;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .purpose b {
    display: inline-block;
    font-weight: inherit;
    will-change: transform, opacity, filter;
  }

  /* The article each rotating fragment was lifted from -- the same
     citation Field.astro shows next to its own fragments, so the
     landing's preview of the field reads as the same system. Absent
     for the manifesto (it's the site's own line, not from an essay)
     and for any "site" fragment, same rule Field.astro already
     follows. */
  .frag-source {
    position: fixed;
    left: 50%;
    top: 74%;
    transform: translate(-50%, 2.6em);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: min(85vw, 34rem);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-align: center;
    color: rgba(227, 175, 149, 0.55);
    z-index: 1;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.8s var(--ease);
  }

  .frag-source.is-visible {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  @keyframes resolve-quiet {
    to { opacity: 1; }
  }

  /* The quote fades out entirely once the field opens -- Field.astro's
     own many fragments take over that role there, so a second,
     smaller decaying-quote widget surviving in the corner would just
     be a redundant, cluttered echo of it. */
  body[data-landing='field'] .purpose,
  body[data-landing='field'] .frag-source {
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 1.2s var(--ease);
  }

  /* background-clip: text has near-universal support now, but has no
     graceful degradation of its own -- a browser without it renders
     these left at color: transparent, i.e. invisible text, not a
     flat fallback color. A design critique's craft checklist called
     this out specifically for .purpose; applied to the wordmark too,
     since it uses the same technique for the same reason. */
  @supports not ((background-clip: text) or (-webkit-background-clip: text)) {
    .wordmark-eduardo {
      background-image: none;
      color: var(--bronze);
    }
    .wordmark-sopalda {
      background-image: none;
      color: var(--gold);
    }
  }

  .meta {
    position: fixed;
    right: clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    bottom: clamp(1.25rem, 5vh, 3rem);
    z-index: 1;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.3em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(227, 175, 149, 0.35);
    text-align: right;
    line-height: 2;
    /* Was invisible (opacity: 0) until the field state -- a design
       critique caught this exactly: "a first visitor never learns
       who or where you are." Location and contact are permanent
       marks, per Landing's own framing above; they should be on the
       first screen, not locked behind the metamorphosis. Same quiet
       resolve-in as the tagline/purpose/cue, not an instant snap. */
    opacity: 0;
    animation: resolve-quiet 1.6s var(--ease) forwards;
    animation-delay: 2.2s;
    transition: opacity 1.4s var(--ease);
  }

  .meta a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(227, 175, 149, 0.25);
  }

  .meta a:hover {
    color: var(--gold);
  }

  body[data-landing='field'] .meta {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* ---- The cue: no word for "enter," just a quiet signal that there's
     more here — same gold-pulse language as the nerve and its stops.
     This is the thing that actually gets someone into the field now;
     nothing times out on its own (see script below), so this has to
     read as clickable without saying so. Pointer move / scroll / touch
     still work too, as a low-friction fallback for anyone who starts
     exploring before noticing it. */
  .cue {
    position: fixed;
    left: 50%;
    bottom: clamp(2.25rem, 8vh, 4rem);
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    z-index: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0.9rem;
    margin: -0.9rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    opacity: 0;
    animation: resolve-quiet 1.6s var(--ease) forwards;
    animation-delay: 2.6s;
    transition: opacity 1.4s var(--ease);
  }

  .cue-line {
    width: 1px;
    height: 2.1rem;
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, var(--gold));
  }

  .cue-dot {
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--gold);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0.7rem 0.15rem rgba(247, 197, 106, 0.5);
    /* Was 2.6s with a scale(1.3) pump on top of the opacity fade --
       busier and faster than the reference's own cue, which just
       breathes opacity slowly (5.6s, no scale). Matched to that; the
       scale pump read as more "attention-grabbing UI widget" than the
       quiet, patient signal this is meant to be. */
    animation: cue-breathe 5.6s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes cue-breathe {
    50% { opacity: 0.28; }
  }

  /* A gold dot with no word is a decoration, not a verb -- a design
     critique's exact phrasing. Reusing "Continue", the word already
     chosen for the button's own aria-label, rather than inventing new
     copy: this just makes the existing label visible to sighted
     visitors too, in the same quiet tracked-mono language as the
     tagline. */
  .cue-word {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.34em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(230, 203, 155, 0.55);
  }

  .cue:focus-visible {
    outline: 1.5px solid var(--gold);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  body[data-landing='field'] .cue {
    animation: none;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .mark,
    .wordmark-eduardo,
    .wordmark-sopalda,
    .tagline,
    .purpose,
    .meta,
    .cue {
      transition: none;
    }
    .wordmark-eduardo,
    .wordmark-sopalda,
    .tagline,
    .purpose,
    .cue {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 1;
      filter: none;
    }
    /* Letters are revealed via JS (Web Animations API), not this
       stylesheet -- the script checks prefers-reduced-motion itself
       and uses a near-instant duration there, but these act as a
       belt-and-braces default in case a letter is ever visible before
       that animation has actually run. */
    /* Not .wordmark-sopalda b here -- same scoping reason as the
       comment above .wordmark-sopalda: a rule here could never match
       those runtime-created letters anyway. Its script already
       collapses the reveal to a 1ms animation and skips the dock
       wander entirely when reduced is true, which is the real
       safety net for that element. */
    .purpose b {
      opacity: 1;
      filter: none;
      transform: none;
    }
    .frag-source {
      transition: none;
    }
    .cue-dot {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { FRAGS } from '../data/fragments';

  // ================= sopalda: per-letter reveal + scatter-on-dock =================
  // A second opinion's reference prototype (sopaldalanding.zip) gives the
  // surname its own distinct animation -- letters resolve in individually,
  // then take a wandering detour before settling into the corner mark,
  // "sopalda ~6.8s, con un desvío" in the reference's own notes. EDUARDO
  // stays as a single block (the reference's own given name is more
  // direct too), matching this file's established theme: EDUARDO
  // resolves first, sopalda gathers a half-beat behind.
  //
  // Splitting into per-letter <b>s to animate them independently means
  // background-clip: text on the parent has nothing to clip any more
  // (see the CSS comment on .wordmark-sopalda b) -- each letter carries
  // its own slice of the same gradient instead, sized and positioned in
  // PERCENTAGES (not fixed pixels) so the slice stays correctly aligned
  // as font-size continuously shrinks through the whole dock transition,
  // not just at the two endpoints.
  function initWordmarkSopalda(reduced: boolean): () => void {
    const el = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.wordmark-sopalda');
    if (!el) return () => {};
    const text = el.textContent || '';
    el.textContent = '';
    // Hardcoded, not read from getComputedStyle -- the CSS rule for
    // .wordmark-sopalda deliberately carries no background-image any
    // more (it has no direct text left to clip; see that rule's own
    // comment), so computed style would just return "none" here.
    // Keep this in sync with that comment's gradient stops by hand if
    // they ever change.
    //
    // A near-white nacar-sheen spike used to sit at the peak here, a
    // "specular highlight" for brushed metal -- called out directly as
    // looking like a cheap TV chrome effect rather than something
    // precious. Plain gold peak instead, no hard flash.
    const gradient =
      'linear-gradient(100deg, var(--bronze-deep) 0%, var(--bronze) 42%, var(--gold) 50%, var(--bronze) 58%, var(--bronze-deep) 100%)';
    const letters = [...text].map((ch) => {
      const b = document.createElement('b');
      b.textContent = ch;
      // Set directly rather than relying on the .wordmark-sopalda b
      // CSS rule -- Astro scopes this component's <style> block by
      // stamping a data-astro-cid attribute onto template elements at
      // build time, but these <b>s are created here, at runtime, and
      // never get that attribute, so the scoped rule silently never
      // matches them. Confirmed directly: computed style showed plain
      // black text on a literal rectangular gradient background
      // (background-clip sitting at its default border-box) instead
      // of clipped, transparent text -- the exact failure mode
      // Field.astro's own header comment already documents for this
      // same class of bug.
      b.style.display = 'inline-block';
      b.style.color = 'transparent';
      b.style.backgroundClip = 'text';
      (b.style as any).webkitBackgroundClip = 'text';
      b.style.backgroundImage = gradient;
      b.style.opacity = '0';
      b.style.filter = 'blur(14px)';
      el.appendChild(b);
      return b;
    });

    function sliceGradient() {
      const total = el!.getBoundingClientRect().width;
      let offset = 0;
      letters.forEach((b) => {
        const w = b.getBoundingClientRect().width;
        const r = total / w;
        const f = offset / total;
        const posPct = r <= 1 ? 0 : (100 * f * r) / (r - 1);
        b.style.backgroundSize = `${r * 100}% 100%`;
        b.style.backgroundPosition = `${posPct}% 0`;
        offset += w;
      });
    }

    function reveal() {
      sliceGradient();
      letters.forEach((b, i) => {
        b.animate(
          [
            { opacity: 0, transform: 'translateY(14px)', filter: 'blur(14px)' },
            { opacity: 1, transform: 'translateY(0)', filter: 'blur(0)' },
          ],
          {
            duration: reduced ? 1 : 1400,
            delay: reduced ? 0 : 900 + i * 55,
            easing: 'cubic-bezier(.22,.7,.2,1)',
            fill: 'forwards',
          }
        );
      });
    }

    // The slice math needs real layout (getBoundingClientRect), so it
    // has to run after the browser has actually laid the letters out --
    // a font load or a resize can change that width, so it's redone
    // then too, not just once on the first frame.
    requestAnimationFrame(reveal);
    let resizeT: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;
    addEventListener('resize', () => {
      clearTimeout(resizeT);
      resizeT = setTimeout(sliceGradient, 150);
    });

    let gathered = false;
    return function gather() {
      if (reduced || gathered) return;
      gathered = true;
      letters.forEach((b, i) => {
        b.getAnimations().forEach((a) => a.cancel());
        const wx = (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * 46;
        const wy = (Math.random() > 0.5 ? -1 : 1) * (30 + Math.random() * 34);
        const wr = (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * 14;
        b.animate(
          [
            { transform: 'translate(0, 0) rotate(0deg)', offset: 0 },
            { transform: `translate(${wx}px, ${wy}px) rotate(${wr}deg)`, offset: 0.5 },
            { transform: 'translate(0, 0) rotate(0deg)', offset: 1 },
          ],
          {
            duration: 3200,
            delay: i * 90,
            easing: 'cubic-bezier(.18,.2,.2,1)',
            fill: 'both',
          }
        );
      });
      // The slice is sized against sopalda's CURRENT (hero) width; the
      // dock transition keeps shrinking font-size for another 4.2s
      // after this, so a few more re-slices during that window keep
      // the gradient aligned instead of freezing it at the hero ratio
      // (which -- being a ratio between letters, not an absolute size
      // -- is actually constant throughout, but re-measuring costs
      // nothing and removes any doubt from sub-pixel rounding).
      [200, 800, 1800, 3200].forEach((t) => setTimeout(sliceGradient, t));
    };
  }

  function initLanding() {
    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    // No real LinkedIn URL yet — stub the click rather than send anyone
    // to a placeholder href.
    document.getElementById('li')?.addEventListener('click', (e) => e.preventDefault());

    const gatherSopalda = initWordmarkSopalda(reduced);

    let stopped = false;
    function enterField() {
      document.body.dataset.landing = 'field';
      // The quote itself is Field.astro's job from here on -- stop
      // scheduling anything new rather than let a hidden decay cycle
      // keep running behind the field for no visible reason.
      stopped = true;
      gatherSopalda();
    }

    if (reduced) {
      enterField();
    } else {
      // Deliberate only — nothing times out and forces this on its own,
      // and plain pointer movement doesn't count: someone just moving
      // the mouse to read isn't choosing to continue. The cue is the
      // intended path; scroll and touch are the low-friction fallback,
      // since both are already an act of trying to move past this screen.
      document.getElementById('cue')?.addEventListener('click', enterField);
      addEventListener('wheel', enterField, { once: true, passive: true });
      addEventListener('touchstart', enterField, { once: true, passive: true });
    }

    initQuote(reduced, () => stopped);
  }

  // ================= the hero quote: reveal, rest, decay, repeat =================
  // Ported from a second opinion's reference prototype (sopaldalanding.zip),
  // adapted to this site's two-layer #frag-a/#frag-b markup, its own
  // fragment pool (src/data/fragments.ts), and a #frag-source citation
  // line the reference didn't have.
  function initQuote(reduced: boolean, isStopped: () => boolean) {
    const layers = [document.getElementById('frag-a'), document.getElementById('frag-b')].filter(
      (el): el is HTMLParagraphElement => el instanceof HTMLParagraphElement
    );
    const sourceEl = document.getElementById('frag-source');
    if (layers.length < 2) return;

    // The manifesto is the site's own thesis, not an article fragment --
    // shown first, every time, with no citation. Everything after it
    // comes from the same pool Field.astro draws from.
    const MANIFESTO = { text: 'Understand it first… then you decide if you trust it.', source: null as string | null };

    let queue: number[] = [];
    function shuffled(n: number) {
      const arr = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
      for (let i = arr.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
        [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
      }
      return arr;
    }
    function next(): { text: string; source: string | null } {
      if (queue.length === 0) queue = shuffled(FRAGS.length);
      const f = FRAGS[queue.pop()!];
      return { text: f.text, source: f.source };
    }

    function buildLetters(node: HTMLElement, text: string): HTMLElement[] {
      node.innerHTML = '';
      const made: HTMLElement[] = [];
      text.split(/(\s+)/).forEach((tok) => {
        if (/^\s+$/.test(tok)) {
          node.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' '));
          return;
        }
        const word = document.createElement('span');
        word.className = 'word';
        [...tok].forEach((ch) => {
          const b = document.createElement('b');
          b.textContent = ch;
          b.style.opacity = '0';
          word.appendChild(b);
          made.push(b);
        });
        node.appendChild(word);
      });
      return made;
    }

    let live = 0;
    let letters: HTMLElement[] = [];
    let held = false;
    let decaying = false;

    function revealSourceFor(source: string | null) {
      if (!sourceEl) return;
      if (!source) {
        sourceEl.classList.remove('is-visible');
        return;
      }
      sourceEl.textContent = `— ${source}`;
      // A beat behind the quote itself, same relationship as
      // Field.astro's own quote/citation pairing.
      setTimeout(() => sourceEl.classList.add('is-visible'), reduced ? 0 : 900);
    }

    function setLine(text: string, source: string | null, node: HTMLElement) {
      decaying = false;
      node.hidden = false;
      node.classList.toggle('is-manifesto', text === MANIFESTO.text);
      const made = buildLetters(node, text);
      letters = made;
      revealSourceFor(source);
      made.forEach((b, i) => {
        b.animate(
          [
            { opacity: 0, transform: 'translateY(10px)', filter: 'blur(8px)' },
            { opacity: 1, transform: 'none', filter: 'blur(0)' },
          ],
          {
            duration: reduced ? 1 : 1600,
            delay: reduced ? 0 : 90 + i * 42,
            easing: 'cubic-bezier(.22,.7,.2,1)',
            fill: 'forwards',
          }
        );
      });
    }

    // ---- decay: the same physical argument as Field.astro's own
    // fragment decay, just applied to the one quote a visitor sees
    // before they've even entered the field. Four modes, cycling in
    // order, never repeating back-to-back. ----
    const DEATHS = ['float', 'fall', 'explode', 'corrode'] as const;
    let deathI = 0;
    type Particle = {
      el: HTMLElement; x: number; y: number; px: number; py: number;
      rot: number; rotv: number; damp: number; life: number; fade: number;
      wake: number; scale: number; link: boolean; rest: number; breakAt: number;
    };
    let phys: Particle[] = [];
    let physMode: (typeof DEATHS)[number] | '' = '';
    let physT = 0;
    let physRunning = false;
    let physLast = 0;
    let physRaf = 0;

    function stopPhys() {
      physRunning = false;
      phys = [];
      if (physRaf) cancelAnimationFrame(physRaf);
    }

    function stepPhys(now: number) {
      if (!physRunning || held) return;
      const dt = Math.min(0.032, (now - physLast) / 1000) || 0.016;
      physLast = now;
      physT += dt;
      const wind = Math.sin(physT * 0.7) * 22;

      if (physMode === 'corrode') {
        phys.forEach((p) => {
          if (p.link && physT > p.breakAt) p.link = false;
        });
        for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
          for (let i = 0; i < phys.length - 1; i++) {
            const p = phys[i], q = phys[i + 1];
            if (!p.link) continue;
            const dx = q.x - p.x, dy = q.y - p.y;
            const d = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;
            const corr = (d - p.rest) * 0.18;
            const nx = dx / d, ny = dy / d;
            p.x += nx * corr * 0.5; p.y += ny * corr * 0.5;
            q.x -= nx * corr * 0.5; q.y -= ny * corr * 0.5;
          }
        }
      }

      phys.forEach((p) => {
        if (physT < p.wake) return;
        let ax = 0, ay = 0;
        if (physMode === 'fall') ay = 380;
        if (physMode === 'float') { ay = -28; ax = wind * 0.7; }
        if (physMode === 'explode') ay = 12;
        if (physMode === 'corrode') ay = 12;

        const vx = p.x - p.px, vy = p.y - p.py;
        const nx = p.x + vx * p.damp + ax * dt * dt;
        const ny = p.y + vy * p.damp + ay * dt * dt;
        p.px = p.x; p.py = p.y;
        p.x = nx; p.y = ny;
        p.rot += p.rotv * dt * 60;
        p.life -= p.fade * dt * 60;
        if (physMode === 'explode') p.scale = 1 + Math.min(0.55, physT * 0.35);
        p.el.style.transform = `translate(${p.x}px, ${p.y}px) rotate(${p.rot}deg) scale(${p.scale || 1})`;
        p.el.style.opacity = String(Math.max(0, p.life));
        if (physMode === 'corrode') {
          const rust = Math.min(1, physT / 7);
          p.el.style.filter = `blur(${rust * 4.5}px)`;
          (p.el.style as any).color = rust > 0.35 ? '#2a160e' : '';
        }
      });
      physRaf = requestAnimationFrame(stepPhys);
    }

    function decay() {
      if (reduced || held || decaying || isStopped()) return;
      decaying = true;
      const mode = DEATHS[deathI % DEATHS.length];
      deathI += 1;
      physMode = mode;
      physT = 0;
      physLast = performance.now();

      const origin = letters.map((b) => b.getBoundingClientRect());
      phys = letters.map((b, i) => {
        b.getAnimations().forEach((a) => a.cancel());
        const ang = (i / Math.max(letters.length, 1)) * Math.PI * 2 + (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.35;
        const speed = mode === 'explode' ? 11 + Math.random() * 8 : 2.4 + Math.random() * 2;
        let px = 0, py = 0;
        if (mode === 'explode') {
          px = -Math.cos(ang) * speed;
          py = -Math.sin(ang) * speed;
        }
        const rest = origin[i + 1] ? origin[i + 1].left - origin[i].left : origin[i].width;
        return {
          el: b, x: 0, y: 0, px, py, rot: 0,
          rotv: mode === 'explode' ? (Math.random() - 0.5) * 14 : (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.4,
          damp: mode === 'float' ? 0.965 : mode === 'fall' ? 0.996 : mode === 'explode' ? 0.986 : 0.978,
          life: 1,
          fade: mode === 'explode' ? 0.0055 : mode === 'fall' ? 0.0026 : mode === 'float' ? 0.0018 : 0.0022,
          wake: mode === 'explode' ? 0.28 : mode === 'float' ? i * 0.12 : mode === 'corrode' ? i * 0.16 : mode === 'fall' ? i * 0.05 : 0,
          scale: 1,
          link: mode === 'corrode' && i < letters.length - 1,
          rest,
          breakAt: 0.35 + i * 0.16,
        };
      });
      physRunning = true;
      physRaf = requestAnimationFrame(stepPhys);
      if (sourceEl) sourceEl.classList.remove('is-visible');

      const dying = layers[live];
      const nextLayer = layers[1 - live];
      live = 1 - live;
      const overlap = mode === 'explode' ? 3200 : 4200;
      const clearAfter = mode === 'explode' ? 10000 : 14000;
      setTimeout(() => {
        if (isStopped()) return;
        const f = next();
        setLine(f.text, f.source, nextLayer);
        setTimeout(scheduleDecay, 14000);
      }, overlap);
      setTimeout(() => {
        if (dying !== layers[live]) {
          dying.innerHTML = '';
          dying.hidden = true;
        }
      }, clearAfter);
    }

    function scheduleDecay() {
      if (reduced || isStopped()) return;
      decay();
    }

    // Touching the quote holds it against its own death -- the
    // reference prototype's own framing ("tocarla la sostiene, para la
    // muerte"). Once held, it stays held: nothing brings a death cycle
    // back on its own, same as the reference (only re-entering fresh,
    // i.e. a reload, resets it).
    layers.forEach((node) => {
      node.addEventListener('pointerdown', () => {
        held = true;
        stopPhys();
        letters.forEach((b) => {
          b.getAnimations().forEach((a) => a.cancel());
          b.style.transition = 'transform 1.1s ease, opacity 1.1s ease, color 1.1s ease, filter 1.1s ease';
          b.style.transform = 'none';
          b.style.opacity = '1';
          (b.style as any).color = '';
          b.style.filter = 'none';
        });
      });
    });

    setLine(MANIFESTO.text, MANIFESTO.source, layers[0]);
    if (!reduced) setTimeout(scheduleDecay, 14000);
  }

  initLanding();
</script>
